config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1
	bool "Step S1: the workload on Zephyr threads and a message queue"
	depends on MULTITHREADING
	# The adapter zone builds absolute deadlines (K_TIMEOUT_ABS_TICKS), which need 64-bit kernel
	# timeouts. Zephyr turns them on by default, and the Crux Thread and Queue providers selected
	# them while the workload called those providers. The product asks for them itself now.
	select TIMEOUT_64BIT
	help
	  Run the workload: a producer and a consumer thread joined by one message queue, created by
	  the adapter zone app/os with Zephyr kernel calls, reading Crux Time and reporting through
	  Crux Logging. Steps S2 and S3 build on this one. The product uses no Crux Thread, Sync or
	  Queue provider, so none is selected for it.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2
	bool "Step S2: send each workload message to a TCP peer"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1 && CRUX_SOCKETS && CRUX_NETIF
	help
	  After a message is logged, open a TCP connection to the peer, send the message
	  and expect the same eight bytes back. The Sockets profile of Crux defines the
	  calls that are used. The exchange is built and never run in Stage 1 CI.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_PEER_ADDRESS
	string "IPv4 address of the TCP peer"
	default "192.168.77.1"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_PEER_PORT
	int "TCP port of the peer"
	default 42420
	range 1 65535
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S3
	bool "Step S3: keep the workload configuration record in a file"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1 && CRUX_FILE_FATFS_RT1060
	help
	  At start, mount the microSD volume that the devicetree entry controller_storage
	  describes, bind its files directory to Crux File, load the configuration record from
	  the file config.rec and use it, or save the default record when none exists. The image
	  creates no directory and never formats a volume: the directory "files" must already
	  exist on the card, and a card without it leaves the default configuration in use, as
	  does a volume that does not mount. Built and never run in Stage 1 CI.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S4
	bool "Step S4: report the reset cause and feed a watchdog from the workload cycle"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1 && CRUX_SYSTEM && WATCHDOG && HWINFO && REBOOT
	help
	  At start, write one line for the reset cause through Crux Logging and clear the flags, then
	  start the one watchdog of the boot with an interval of about ten workload cycles and feed it
	  once for each workload cycle, from the workload's producer thread and nowhere else. The
	  application uses <crux/system.h> only. The native watchdog, HWINFO and reboot facilities,
	  and the devicetree alias crux-watchdog0 (boards/s4.overlay), are what Crux System requires of
	  the image; the product selects no Crux symbol. A platform without a watchdog, such as the
	  native_sim simulator, reports it as not supported and the workload runs without one. A missed
	  feed ends in the watchdog's own reset; the product adds none. Built and never run on a board.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_WORKER_STACK
	int "Stack bytes of each workload thread"
	default 4096
	range 1024 65536
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1
	help
	  Each of the two workload threads has a stack of this size. The default is what the Crux
	  Thread provider gave the workload before it called Zephyr directly, and the S2 image sets
	  8192 because its exchange reaches into the network stack.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
